# Worked solutions, chapter 1 (modeling and block diagrams). number -> (answer, concept, solution)
S = {
"1.1": ("A", "Feedback compares output with input and corrects the error.", "Closed-loop systems are more accurate than open-loop ones because the feedback path feeds the output back to be compared with the input, and the resulting error is corrected. So $A$ is true, and $R$ is exactly why.\n\nBoth true and $R$ explains $A$: **(a)**."),
"1.2": ("A", "Effects of feedback: reduces error, changes gain with frequency, can destabilise.", "1. Negative feedback reduces the error: true.\n2. Loop gain varies with frequency, so feedback raises the effective gain in some bands and lowers it in others: true.\n3. A stable open-loop system can become unstable once the loop is closed (high gain, delay): true.\n\nAll three are correct: **(a)**."),
"1.3": ("A", "Mason's gain formula $T=\\frac{1}{\\Delta}\\sum P_k\\Delta_k$.", "Forward paths: $P_1=rsu$ and $P_2=efh$.\nLoops: $L_1=st$ and $L_2=fg$, which do not touch each other.\n\n$\\Delta=1-st-fg+stfg=(1-st)(1-fg)$.\n$P_1$ touches $L_1$ only, so $\\Delta_1=1-fg$. $P_2$ touches $L_2$ only, so $\\Delta_2=1-st$.\n\n$T=\\dfrac{rsu(1-fg)+efh(1-st)}{(1-st)(1-fg)}=\\dfrac{rsu}{1-st}+\\dfrac{efh}{1-fg}$, which is **(a)**."),
"1.4": ("D", "Properties of feedback.", "1. Accuracy *can* be improved by adjusting the loop gain: statement is false.\n2. Overall gain becomes $G/(1+GH)$, which is lower: true.\n3. Sensor noise enters the loop and reduces accuracy: true.\n4. Feedback can cause instability: true.\n\nOnly 2, 3 and 4: **(d)**."),
"1.5": (None, "Step response $\\to$ transfer function: $G=sC(s)$; cascade means multiply.", "$C(s)=-0.5\\left(\\dfrac1s+\\dfrac1{s+2}\\right)$, so $G_1=sC(s)=-\\dfrac{s+1}{s+2}$.\nCascading with $G_2=\\dfrac1{s+1}$ gives $G_1G_2=\\dfrac{-1}{s+2}$.\n\n**This result is not among the options**, so the question as transcribed has an error (probably in the step response, e.g. $0.5(1-e^{-2t})$ would give option (a)). The answer key is therefore left unset for this question."),
"1.6": ("C", "Zero initial conditions mean no stored energy.", "Initial conditions are the energy stored in the system at $t=0$. Zero initial conditions mean the system is at rest with no energy stored in any element: **(c)**."),
"1.7": ("C", "$\\mathcal L\\{g(t)\\}=G(s)$.", "The transfer function is the Laplace transform of the impulse response:\n$G(s)=\\mathcal L\\{e^{-t}\\}=\\dfrac1{s+1}$, which is **(c)**."),
"1.8": ("A", "Back-emf adds damping.", "Back-emf is proportional to speed and opposes the applied voltage, like viscous friction without the energy being stored. It adds damping, so it improves stability: **(a)**."),
"1.9": ("A", "Final value theorem / decay of a stable impulse response.", "$g(t)=e^{-t}\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$. Equivalently $\\lim_{s\\to0}s\\cdot\\frac1{s+1}=0$. So the steady-state value is **zero (a)**."),
"1.10": ("A", "Sensitivity of closed-loop gain to $G$.", "$M=\\dfrac{G}{1+GH}$.\n$S_G^M=\\dfrac{\\partial M/M}{\\partial G/G}=\\dfrac{1}{1+GH}$, which is **(a)**."),
"1.11": ("C", "$Y(s)=H(s)\\cdot\\frac1s$ for a unit step.", "$Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{te^{-t}\\}=\\dfrac1{(s+1)^2}$.\n$H(s)=sY(s)=\\dfrac{s}{(s+1)^2}$, which is **(c)**."),
"1.12": ("D", "Initial value theorem: $f(0^+)=\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}sF(s)$.", "$sF(s)=\\dfrac5{s^2+3s+2}\\to0$ as $s\\to\\infty$. So $f(0^+)=0$: **(d)**."),
"1.13": ("A", "Superposition: set $R=0$ and find $Y/d$.", "With $R=0$ the error signal is $-Y$. The summing junction output is $-3Y+d$.\n$Y=\\dfrac{2}{3s+1}(d-3Y)\\Rightarrow Y\\left(1+\\dfrac{6}{3s+1}\\right)=\\dfrac{2d}{3s+1}$.\n$\\dfrac{Y}{d}=\\dfrac{2}{3s+7}$, which is **(a)**."),
"1.14": ("B", "Impulse response is the inverse Laplace transform of the transfer function.", "$\\dfrac{C}{R}=\\dfrac1{s(s+1)}=\\dfrac1s-\\dfrac1{s+1}$.\n$c(t)=1-e^{-t}$ for $t\\ge0$: **(b)**."),
"1.15": ("A", "Torque $T=P/\\omega$.", "$\\omega=\\dfrac{2\\pi\\cdot900}{60}=94.25$ rad/s.\n$T=\\dfrac{10\\,000}{94.25}\\approx106$ N-m: **(a)**."),
"1.16": ("A", "Minimum-phase transfer functions have no RHP poles or zeros.", "A transfer function with no poles or zeros in the right half of the $s$-plane is *by definition* called minimum phase. Statement (I) describes the property and (II) names it, so (II) correctly explains (I): **(a)**."),
"1.17": ("B", "Unit conversion between rpm and rad/s.", "$1000\\ \\text{rpm}=\\dfrac{2\\pi\\cdot1000}{60}=104.72$ rad/s.\n$K_t=\\dfrac{5}{104.72}=0.0477\\approx0.048$ V/(rad/s): **(b)**."),
"1.18": ("D", "Unity feedback: $T=\\dfrac{G}{1+G}\\Rightarrow G=\\dfrac{T}{1-T}$.", "$G=\\dfrac{100}{s^2+8s+100-100}=\\dfrac{100}{s^2+8s}$: **(d)**."),
"1.19": ("C", "Laplace transform with frequency shifting.", "$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin at\\}=\\dfrac a{s^2+a^2}$ with $a=2\\omega$, and $e^{-2t}$ shifts $s\\to s+2$.\n$\\mathcal L=\\dfrac{2\\omega}{(s+2)^2+4\\omega^2}$: **(c)**."),
"1.20": ("A", "Rate (derivative) feedback on the output.", "Feeding back the rate of the output measured by a tachogenerator gives velocity feedback, which is used to limit the speed of the motor, per the standard key: **(a)**."),
"1.21": ("D", "A tachogenerator measures speed.", "A tachogenerator produces a voltage proportional to shaft speed, i.e. the derivative of position. It is the device used for derivative (velocity) feedback in a servo system: **(d)**."),
"1.22": ("A", "Minimum-phase systems.", "A stable system whose poles and zeros all lie in the left half of the $s$-plane is a minimum-phase transfer function: **(a)**."),
"1.23": ("C", "Dominant poles lie closest to the $j\\omega$ axis.", "The dominant pole is the one nearest the origin, i.e. the lowest-frequency pole, because its response decays slowest and controls the transient. Among the options this is **(c)**."),
"1.24": ("B", "Signal flow graphs apply to linear systems only and are not unique.", "1. A signal flow graph represents *linear* systems only: false.\n2. Different sets of equations (and node choices) give different graphs for the same system: true.\n\nOnly 2: **(b)**."),
"1.25": ("A", "Closed-loop time constant from a first-order loop.", "$\\dfrac{C}{R}=\\dfrac{10K}{1+10s+10K}=\\dfrac{10K/(1+10K)}{1+\\frac{10}{1+10K}s}$.\nTime constant $\\tau_{cl}=\\dfrac{10}{1+10K}$. It must be $10/20=0.5$.\n$1+10K=20\\Rightarrow K=1.9$: **(a)**."),
"1.26": ("B", "Reduce the diagram; check the feedback signal.", "Let $e=R-F$. The forward path gives $C=\\dfrac{e}{s+1}$, and the lower path gives $\\dfrac{e}{s+1}$ too.\nThe lower summing junction forms $F=C-\\dfrac{e}{s+1}=0$.\nSo $e=R$ and $\\dfrac CR=\\dfrac1{s+1}$: **(b)**."),
"1.27": ("A", "Feedback trades gain for sensitivity.", "The closed-loop gain is $G/(1+GH)$, lower than the open-loop gain by $1+GH$. Better sensitivity is paid for by loss of gain: **(a)**."),
"1.28": ("A", "Characteristic equation: $1+G(s)H(s)=0$.", "$1+\\dfrac{K(s+2)}{(s+1)(s+4)}=0\\Rightarrow(s+1)(s+4)+K(s+2)=0$: **(a)**."),
"1.29": ("C", "Signal flow graphs come from algebraic equations.", "A signal flow graph is drawn from the set of algebraic equations (in the Laplace domain) that express cause-and-effect between variables: **(c)**."),
"1.30": ("D", "What feedback is used to control.", "Feedback is used to control sensitivity to parameter changes, the effect of internal disturbances, and distortion in nonlinear systems. All three: **(d)**."),
"1.31": ("A", "Control system terminology.", "The subsystem that produces the input to the plant is the controller: **(a)**."),
"1.32": ("D", "Open-loop from closed-loop: $G=\\dfrac{T}{1-T}$.", "$G=\\dfrac{s+4}{s^2+7s+13-s-4}=\\dfrac{s+4}{s^2+6s+9}$.\nDC gain $G(0)=\\dfrac49$: **(d)**."),
}
